# Caddyfile — static origin for mastermind-x.com, behind the TencentEdgeOne CDN
# (migrated from Cloudflare — the CF-specific notes below now mean "the CDN edge").
#
# The CDN terminates public TLS at its edge and connects back to this origin.
# `tls internal` serves Caddy's self-signed cert on :443 so the edge<->origin hop
# is still encrypted; the CDN's origin-fetch/SSL mode must ACCEPT a self-signed
# cert (Cloudflare's "Full", or EdgeOne's equivalent non-strict HTTPS origin pull).
# To upgrade later, replace `tls internal` with a CDN Origin Certificate
# (`tls /path/cert.pem /path/key.pem`) and switch the edge to strict verification.
#
# HTTPS ENFORCEMENT: Force-HTTPS is the EDGE's job — enable "Force HTTPS" + HSTS in
# the EdgeOne console. Cloudflare used to redirect http->https AND auto-upgrade
# stray mixed content; EdgeOne does neither by default, so that toggle is now what
# stops http://mastermind-x.com from serving plaintext. This origin deliberately
# does NOT redirect (an origin redirect behind a CDN can loop the edge's origin
# pull — see disable_redirects below); it only ADDS the HSTS response header on the
# site block, which is safe to emit from behind the CDN and hardens return visits.
# Until the edge toggle is on, :80 serves the SAME site as :443 (mirror block
# below) — never Caddy's implicit empty 200, which the edge can cache for the
# httpS URLs too (the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning; scheme-less cache key).
#
# Pre-launch: the whole site is served but marked noindex so it can't be crawled
# before launch. Product login (Supabase) arrives in Slice 2; for a private beta
# right now, front this with Cloudflare Access (email OTP, zero code).

{
	# The CDN edge owns the public http->https redirect for the proxied hosts; don't
	# fight it with Caddy's automatic upgrade, which can loop the edge's origin pull.
	# Only the grey-cloud admin.* host (direct, no CDN) declares its own explicit
	# http:// redirect block below — disable_redirects just suppresses the AUTO ones.
	auto_https disable_redirects
	# Trust Cloudflare's forwarded client IP (so logs show the real visitor).
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

# Shared serving body for mastermind-x.com — imported by BOTH the https site
# block and the plain-HTTP mirror below it, so the two schemes can never drift
# apart. EdgeOne's GET cache key IGNORES the scheme (verified live 2026-07-11
# with a throwaway ?query key): whatever port 80 answers for a path can be
# served from cache to httpS visitors — so :80 must serve the real site with
# the real Cache-Control, never Caddy's implicit empty 200.
(mastermind_static) {
	# Root is /opt/macro/site.served, NOT the git work-tree /opt/macro/site.
	# macro-update rsyncs site/ -> site.served (atomic per-file rename) so a
	# `git reset --hard` refresh can never expose a half-written 0-byte file to be
	# cached by the CDN edge (the 2026-07-03 white-page incident). Paired with the
	# short must-revalidate Cache-Control below as defence-in-depth.
	root * /opt/macro/site.served
	encode zstd gzip

	# Slice 2 backend — FastAPI (macro-api.service) on localhost. Everything under
	# /api/* is proxied here; the rest is served as static files below. Per-user
	# auth lives in the API (Supabase) + (later) a login wall; no shared password.
	reverse_proxy /api/* 127.0.0.1:8000

	# ── Site Access Gate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	#
	# WHAT IT DOES: HTML navigations (the root "/" and any *.html path) are
	# checked against an operator-managed IP/country blocklist by asking the
	# macro-api gate endpoint (/api/gate/check) before serving the file.
	#   • 204 (allow)  → fall through to file_server below (normal serving).
	#   • 403 (block)  → return the 403 response body (the coming-soon page)
	#                    directly to the visitor.
	# Assets (JS, CSS, images, fonts, JSON, etc.) are NOT gated — the visitor
	# who is blocked on HTML navigation cannot load those either, since the
	# coming-soon page is fully self-contained and has no external asset refs.
	#
	# FAIL-OPEN (verified with a local Caddy + stub-gate test — all cases pass):
	#   1. app/gate.py is fail-open (missing/corrupt store → enabled=false → 204).
	#   2. The `route` below asks the gate and CONTINUES to file_server on a 2xx
	#      allow; a 403 copy_response writes the coming-soon page and STOPS, so the
	#      block page keeps its no-store and is never tagged with the max-age header.
	#   3. If the gate upstream is DOWN, the reverse_proxy errors and `handle_errors`
	#      serves the requested static file with a forced 200 — never a 502.
	#
	# DEFAULT STATE IS SAFE: the store file defaults to {enabled:false}, so the gate
	# returns 204 for every visitor on a fresh install (every HTML request does a
	# cheap 204 gate check, then serves normally). A 403 only ever happens when the
	# operator enables the gate AND the visitor matches a blocked rule.
	#
	# WHY ONLY HTML: the coming-soon page is fully self-contained (no external
	# deps), so gating HTML navigation is sufficient — a blocked visitor sees the
	# coming-soon page and nothing else. Assets stay ungated (one hop per pageview).
	#
	# This file is re-installed by macro-update on every main commit. Do NOT
	# "simplify" the route / handle_errors shape without re-running the fail-open
	# test — the earlier `handle` + `handle_response @blocked` shape shipped inert
	# (the gate was never consulted). See app/deploy/SITE_GATE.md.

	@gate_html {
		# Match the root and .html paths; exclude /api/* (already proxied above).
		path / *.html
		not path /api/*
	}

	handle @gate_html {
		route {
			# Ask the gate about THIS request. `method GET` + `rewrite` turn the
			# sub-request into GET /api/gate/check while preserving the visitor's
			# headers (EO-Client-IPCountry / EO-Connecting-IP / CF-*) so the gate
			# can read them.
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/gate/check

				# 2xx (204 allow): empty handle_response → CONTINUE to file_server.
				@allow status 2xx
				handle_response @allow {
				}
				# Non-2xx (403 block): the default copy_response streams the gate's
				# body (the self-contained coming-soon page) + its no-store/X-Gate
				# headers back to the visitor and STOPS — so the max-age header
				# below never touches the block response (it keeps no-store).
			}
			# Reached ONLY on allow — serve the file with the normal short HTML TTL.
			header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	handle_errors {
		# FAIL-OPEN: if the gate upstream (macro-api) is DOWN, the reverse_proxy
		# above errors; serve the requested static HTML with a forced 200 instead
		# of a 502 white-page (which EdgeOne could surface as its own error page).
		# Scoped to gated HTML paths; every other error keeps Caddy's default
		# handling, and a genuinely missing file still yields its natural 404.
		@gate_html_err {
			path / *.html
			not path /api/*
		}
		handle @gate_html_err {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			file_server {
				status 200
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	# Non-HTML assets (and anything not matched above) — served directly.
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	# --- CDN cache safety (2026-07-03 white-page incident) ----------------------
	# A response with NO Cache-Control gets EdgeOne's LONG default TTL, so any
	# transient bad body would be STUCK at the edge for hours. A short,
	# must-revalidate TTL caps any bad edge entry (HTML <=60s, other static <=300s)
	# and forces the edge to revalidate against the (healthy) origin. Mirrors the
	# greydeercapital.com block below. The zero-window structural fix (atomic
	# swap + flock + 0-byte rsync guard) lives in update.sh.
	#
	# NOTE: HTML's max-age=60 is now set INSIDE the Site Access Gate handle above
	# (on the allow path only), so a blocked visitor's coming-soon page keeps its
	# Cache-Control: no-store instead of being clobbered to a cacheable 60s. Do not
	# re-add a blanket `header @html Cache-Control` here — it would re-cache blocks.
	@static {
		path /vendor/* *.js *.css *.json *.svg *.png *.jpg *.jpeg *.ico *.webp *.woff *.woff2
		not path /api/*
		not path /plotly-2.32.0.min.js
	}
	header @static Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# Version-PINNED vendor bundles are safe to cache long: the filename embeds
	# the version, so its content can never change under the same URL — the
	# white-page-incident concern (bad body stuck at the edge under a reused
	# name) does not apply. Before this, plotly (1.15MB gzipped) was
	# re-revalidated every 300s by every visitor of every chart page.
	@vendor_pinned {
		path /plotly-2.32.0.min.js
	}
	header @vendor_pinned Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	header {
		# PUBLIC (was pre-launch noindex): the site is now open to search engines.
		# The old `X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"` header was removed
		# here so crawlers can index it (paired with robots.txt + sitemap.xml in
		# site/). Re-add that header to pull the whole site back out of search.
		# Basic hardening.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}
}

www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static

	header {
		# Enforce HTTPS on return visits (the edge should set this too). 1 year,
		# matching admin.*; no includeSubDomains — other subdomains manage their own.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}

	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with the CDN's non-strict HTTPS origin pull.
	tls internal

	# Access logs go to journald (`journalctl -u caddy`). We deliberately do NOT
	# write a file log: the stock caddy.service systemd sandbox only permits writes
	# under /var/lib/caddy, so a /var/log/caddy file sink fails with EACCES and
	# crashes startup. journald is the idiomatic, sandbox-safe sink.
}

# ---- apex mastermind-x.com - DIRECT redirect to the CDN-fronted www --------
# The apex A record points straight at this origin (it CANNOT be a CNAME to the
# EdgeOne edge, because mastermind-x.com also carries the MX for email; a CNAME
# at the apex illegally shadows MX and makes mail flap). So the apex is a direct
# host here: Caddy issues a REAL public Let's Encrypt cert (ACME HTTP-01 on :80
# works because apex A -> this box) and 301s everything to https://www, which
# stays behind EdgeOne. Kept OUT of the www serving block above so EdgeOne's www
# origin-pull (Host: www.mastermind-x.com) can never land on this redirect+loop.
http://mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

mastermind-x.com {
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

# ---- plain-HTTP (:80) mirror — the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning fix ------
# With `auto_https disable_redirects` and https-only site blocks, Caddy answered
# ANY plain-HTTP request for these hosts with its IMPLICIT catch-all: an empty
# 200 with no body and no Cache-Control. EdgeOne's origin pull follows the
# visitor's scheme (Force-HTTPS off at the edge), so one bot GET of
# http://mastermind-x.com/china.html primed the edge with that empty 200 —
# and because the edge cache key ignores the scheme, httpS visitors got the
# dead page for hours (no Cache-Control -> EdgeOne's long default TTL).
# Serving the REAL site on :80 means an http origin pull can only ever cache a
# real page bounded by max-age=60. Deliberately NOT a redirect: if the edge's
# origin-pull were ever http-only, an origin http->https redirect would loop
# (see the header notes above) — the edge console owns Force-HTTPS.
http://www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static
}

# ---- plain-HTTP catch-all — kill Caddy's implicit empty 200 -------------------
# Every OTHER hostname that reaches this box over :80 (app./bot./greydeer/
# slate/maltese/stray Host headers) used to fall into the same implicit empty
# 200 — the exact cacheable-poison shape above, just waiting for a CDN in front
# of any of those hosts. A 421 with no-store is never cacheable, so the port-80
# path can't poison anything again. Explicit http:// blocks (the mastermind
# mirror above, the admin redirect below) outrank this hostless block, and
# Caddy answers ACME HTTP-01 challenges before routing, so admin.* cert
# issuance is unaffected.
http:// {
	header Cache-Control "no-store"
	respond "Misdirected: use https://" 421
}

# ---- app.mastermind-x.com — the Mastermind Terminal (Next.js) ----------------
# The charting SaaS frontend, served by terminal.service (next start) on :3000.
# Needs a Cloudflare DNS A record: app -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
app.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# --- Terminal /data static serving (2026-07-03) ------------------------------
	# Serve the terminal's static data JSONs DIRECTLY from disk at Caddy, bypassing
	# Next.js: `next start` 404s public/ files added AFTER the build (verified live:
	# a rsync'd NEW <SYM>.fund.json 404'd until rebuild), and the fundamentals
	# pipeline rsyncs NEW per-symbol files daily (fund/opts/tx waves). Caddy's
	# file_server has no build manifest — a new file serves on the next request.
	# Cache headers set here (plain header: file_server emits no Cache-Control, so
	# no defer needed). EdgeOne still RefreshHits until an owner console rule exists.
	handle /data/* {
		root * /opt/terminal/terminal/public
		@manifest path /data/manifest.json
		@rest {
			path /data/*
			not path /data/manifest.json
		}
		header @manifest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=60, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		header @rest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=300, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}
	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- bot.mastermind-x.com — Brain BOT serve-only mirror (uvicorn :8001) -------
# The Mastermind Brain dashboard (paper-trading FastAPI), served by
# mastermind.service on 127.0.0.1:8001. The app handles its own auth
# (MASTERMIND_PASSWORD in /opt/mastermind/.env; currently disabled). MUST live
# here — without a site block, Caddy's `tls internal` has no cert for this SNI and
# the Cloudflare<->origin handshake fails with HTTP 525. DNS A record:
# bot -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
bot.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}
	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- admin.mastermind-x.com — the Admin console (python -m admin :8787) -------
# Authenticated control + observability console (admin.service on 127.0.0.1:8787).
# UNLIKE the other hosts this is GREY-CLOUD / DNS-only (A record admin ->
# 146.190.142.17, NOT proxied through Cloudflare), so Caddy provisions a REAL
# Let's Encrypt cert over HTTP-01 (port 80 is kept open to all in ufw by
# firewall-cloudflare.sh's grey-cloud escape hatch) — no `tls internal`, no
# Cloudflare in front. The app enforces password + signed-session auth
# (ADMIN_PASSWORD in /etc/macro-admin.env); Caddy adds anti-framing + HSTS headers.
#
# The global `auto_https disable_redirects` (correct for the CF-fronted hosts)
# would otherwise serve this host over PLAINTEXT http:// with no upgrade — and the
# login posts the shared password. So we add an explicit http:// block that
# force-redirects to https:// (Caddy still answers the ACME HTTP-01 challenge on
# :80 ahead of this redirect, so cert issuance/renewal is unaffected).
http://admin.mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://admin.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

admin.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		-Server
	}
}

# ---- home.greydeercapital.com — Grey Home private smart-home dashboard -------
# The app and all home-device credentials stay on the Mac Studio. A dedicated,
# restricted reverse SSH tunnel exposes only the local web server on loopback
# :47900. Cloudflare terminates public TLS; the origin uses Caddy's internal cert.
home.greydeercapital.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:47900 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()"
		-Server
	}
	tls internal
}

# ---- maltese.greydeercapital.com — 线条小狗 Line-Puppy sticker gallery (static) ----
# A SEPARATE Cloudflare zone (greydeercapital.com) proxied to THIS origin; the static
# files live at /opt/maltese (OUTSIDE this /opt/macro checkout — synced by hand via
# scripts/deploy.sh in ~/Documents/线条小狗_stickers). Like the mastermind-x hosts it
# uses `tls internal` paired with Cloudflare SSL mode "Full" (non-strict origin pull).
# This block MUST live in THIS repo file: macro-update reinstalls /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# from here on every main commit, so a block hand-added on the box alone gets wiped.
# Without a block for this SNI the Cloudflare<->origin handshake fails with HTTP 525.
maltese.greydeercapital.com {
	root * /opt/maltese
	encode zstd gzip
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}
	tls internal
}

# ---- slate.greydeercapital.com — Slate personal task boards + Brain (static PWA) ----
# Same greydeercapital.com Cloudflare zone, proxied to this origin (A record
# slate -> 146.190.142.17, proxied, zone SSL mode "Full"). Files rsync'd to
# /opt/slate by the slate repo's deploy tooling
# (github.com/chriswong6031-creator/slate — push-to-main workflow or
# deploy/deploy.sh); this block only serves them. Fully client-side app
# (localStorage/IndexedDB in the visitor's browser) — the origin holds no user
# data, so no auth here; noindex keeps it out of search. Like the other
# CF-fronted hosts it uses `tls internal`; without this SNI block the
# Cloudflare<->origin handshake fails with HTTP 525.
slate.greydeercapital.com {
	root * /opt/slate
	encode zstd gzip
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	# The service worker script must revalidate on every update check so app
	# updates roll immediately (its precache VERSION is content-hashed).
	@sw path /sw.js
	header @sw Cache-Control "no-cache"
	# file_server has no MIME mapping for .webmanifest; combined with nosniff
	# an untyped manifest breaks PWA installs (same lesson as the
	# greydeercapital.com site.webmanifest bug, 2026-07-02).
	@webmanifest path /manifest.webmanifest
	header @webmanifest Content-Type "application/manifest+json"
	@html path *.html /
	header @html Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
	@static path /css/* /js/* /icons/*
	header @static Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		-Server
	}
	tls internal
}

# ---- notes.mastermind-x.com — Slate personal task boards + Brain (static PWA) ----
# PRIMARY home for Slate (also served at slate.greydeercapital.com from the same
# /opt/slate tree). On the mastermind-x.com zone behind the Tencent EdgeOne CDN:
# CNAME notes -> EdgeOne, origin -> 146.190.142.17 with a NON-STRICT HTTPS origin
# pull (accepts Caddy's self-signed `tls internal` cert). Force HTTPS + HSTS are
# the EdgeOne edge's job (see the header note atop this file). Files are rsync'd to
# /opt/slate by the slate repo's push-to-main deploy; this block only serves them.
# Fully client-side app (localStorage/IndexedDB), so the origin holds no user data;
# noindex keeps it out of search. Without this SNI block the edge<->origin
# handshake fails with HTTP 525.
notes.mastermind-x.com {
	root * /opt/slate
	encode zstd gzip
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	# Service worker must revalidate every update check so app updates roll
	# immediately (its precache VERSION is content-hashed).
	@sw path /sw.js
	header @sw Cache-Control "no-cache"
	# file_server has no MIME mapping for .webmanifest; an untyped manifest breaks
	# PWA installs (same lesson as the greydeercapital.com bug, 2026-07-02).
	@webmanifest path /manifest.webmanifest
	header @webmanifest Content-Type "application/manifest+json"
	@html path *.html /
	header @html Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
	@static path /css/* /js/* /icons/*
	header @static Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		-Server
	}
	tls internal
}

# ---- greydeercapital.com — Grey Deer Capital brand site (static) + intake API ----
# Behind Cloudflare (proxied, SSL mode "Full"): `tls internal` serves a self-signed
# origin cert so the edge<->origin hop is encrypted, matching mastermind-x.com.
# Static files live in /opt/greydeercapital; /api/* is the self-hosted inquiry
# intake service (greydeercapital-intake.service) on loopback :8110.
greydeercapital.com, www.greydeercapital.com {
	root * /opt/greydeercapital
	encode zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy /api/* 127.0.0.1:8110

	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	handle_errors {
		rewrite * /404.html
		file_server
	}

	# Caching — fonts/vendor are content-addressed (immutable); assets daily; HTML short
	@immutable {
		path /assets/fonts/* /assets/vendor/*
	}
	@assets {
		path /assets/*
		not path /assets/fonts/* /assets/vendor/*
	}
	@html {
		path *.html /
	}
	header @immutable Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
	header @assets   Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400"
	header @html     Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# file_server has no MIME mapping for .webmanifest, so it served the manifest
	# with NO Content-Type. Combined with the nosniff header below, iOS Safari
	# refuses to type it, downloads it as "document.txt", and the page renders
	# white (2026-07-02 mobile bug). Serve the spec Content-Type explicitly.
	@webmanifest path /site.webmanifest
	header @webmanifest Content-Type "application/manifest+json"

	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		# No third-party origins remain after font self-hosting (2026-07-01).
		# 'unsafe-inline' required: head bootstrap script + inline style attrs.
		Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()"
		-Server
	}

	tls internal
}

# ---- scan.mastermind-x.com — LookScanned (Next.js standalone :3200) -----------
# Separate SaaS (repo: lookscanned-saas). Served by lookscanned.service — a Next.js
# standalone server on 127.0.0.1:3200. Same convention as app.*/notes.*: self-signed
# origin cert, TLS terminated at the EdgeOne CDN edge which origin-pulls over
# (non-strict) HTTPS. REQUIRES the EdgeOne console origin for `scan` = 146.190.142.17;
# if the edge origin points back at the domain/CDN it loops (cdn-loop 423) and never
# reaches this box (added 2026-07-17 after diagnosing exactly that).
scan.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3200
	header {
		-Server
	}
	tls internal
}
